The Nadeo loader hardcodes is_const: false on every method, so false means UNKNOWN — the same bool-defaults-to-a-lie defect as B125/B131, surviving in the loader's other half


## Found by lane-enum during B130, 2026-09-01, by a RED TEST rather than a sweep

## The defect

The **Nadeo loader hardcodes `is_const: false` on every method**, with a comment
saying "that dump carries no declaration text". So on the Nadeo half, `false`
means **UNKNOWN**, not non-const.

**This is the same `bool`-defaults-to-a-lie defect B125 and B131 fixed on the
Core side, surviving in the loader's other half.** B131's audit could not see it:
that audit checks the Core dump's field PRESENCE, not the Nadeo loader's
hardcoded VALUES. A `MethodInfo` built with a literal `false` is invisible to a
presence audit — see the sibling ticket.

## How it was caught, and why nothing else could have caught it

B130 consumed `is_const` for engine receivers. lane-enum's own B116 test
`external_engine_receiver_never_fires` went red, and reading WHY it was red found
this. Consuming the hardcoded `false` would have fired the const-receiver rule on
**every correctly-written const Nadeo call.**

**The fleet sweep could never have found it.** All 807 engine-receiver sites in
the fleet are Core types — `string`, `Json::Value`, `Reflection::MwClassInfo`,
`vec3`. The Nadeo population has **ZERO exposure** in the corpus, so a fleet zero
there means nothing. This is the exposure hazard, landing on the lane that has
been reporting it all night. Read that entry before designing this ticket's
measurement.

## The payoff is game-proven, which is what makes this worth doing

    const CMwNod@ n; n.MwAddRef();   ->  engine REJECTS

So these are **real errors we are choosing not to report because our data is
absent** — a much sharper statement than B116's "no metadata", and it means
fixing Nadeo constness has a demonstrated benefit rather than a speculative one.

`scripts/probe_engine_const_receiver.py` already has the `nadeo-nonconst` row
ready.

## Deliverable

Parse the Nadeo signature text for the trailing ` const`, the way B124/B125 did
for Core's `decl`. **Check first whether the Nadeo dump carries usable signature
text at all** — the loader comment says it does not, and that comment may be as
stale as `lookup_method_return`'s was. Measure it; do not inherit it.

If the text genuinely is absent, the honest fix is to make the ABSENCE
REPRESENTABLE — `Option<bool>` rather than `bool` — so the rule can distinguish
"known non-const" from "we do not know". That is B131's bool-vs-Option finding
applied, and it is a legitimate outcome on its own.

## Measurement design — the fleet is the WRONG oracle here

**The game probe is the oracle, not the corpus.** Zero Nadeo exposure means a
fleet sweep can only produce an uninterpretable zero. Use
`scripts/probe_engine_const_receiver.py` and report per-construct engine
verdicts with controls, the way B130 did:

- A non-const Nadeo method on a const receiver -> must REJECT.
- A const Nadeo method on a const receiver -> must ACCEPT. Without this row, a
  rule that fires on everything passes.
- A non-const Nadeo method on a NON-const receiver -> must ACCEPT.
- The same three on Core types, as the control that the harness is wired at all.